# A `use` name is a value, and the interpreter decides whether it still means the class by
# asking its current frame only. A name bound in the current frame wins; a name bound in an
# enclosing scope does not. The compiled backend has to reproduce both halves, because a
# program that reads the wrong one prints a class where a number belongs.
use java.lang.Math

# A loop header binds into the frame the body runs in, so the number wins in the body.
for Math in [1, 2]
  print Math
end

# A destructuring assignment binds the same way.
a, Math = [10, 20]
print Math

# The name a `catch` binds shares the handler's frame. Reading the message proves the name
# holds the error and not the class; the position is left out so the line number stays out of
# the expected output.
try
  error("boom")
catch Math
  print Math.message
end

# An outer assignment does not reach into a nested block: the block's own frame has no
# binding, so the class wins there while the outer read still sees the value.
Math = 99
print Math
if true
  print Math.abs(-5)
end
